    "before_sme09_block": "### SME-09 — HT variance and SRSWOR\n**Pages:** horvitz-thompson-variance-and-srswor / horvitz-thompson-variance-and-srswor-examples. **A:** Define $\\Delta_{ij}=\\pi_{ij}-\\pi_i\\pi_j$. If every $\\pi_i>0$, prove $ \\operatorname{Var}_d(\\hat T_{HT})=\\sum_{i,j\\in U}\\Delta_{ij}y_iy_j/(\\pi_i\\pi_j)$. The unbiased pair-inclusion estimate $\\sum_{i,j\\in s}(\\Delta_{ij}/\\pi_{ij})(y_i/\\pi_i)(y_j/\\pi_j)$ requires every $\\pi_{ij}>0$; zero pair support does not license division or imply variance estimability. For fixed sample size, $\\sum_j\\Delta_{ij}=0$ yields the Sen–Yates–Grundy identity $-\\tfrac12\\sum_{i,j}\\Delta_{ij}(y_i/\\pi_i-y_j/\\pi_j)^2$, nonnegative only when $\\Delta_{ij}\\le0$ for all distinct pairs. For SRSWOR of $n$ from $N>1$, derive $\\operatorname{Cov}_d(I_i,I_j)=-n(N-n)/(N^2(N-1))$ for $i\\ne j$ and $\\operatorname{Var}_d(\\bar y)=(1-n/N)S_U^2/n$, where $S_U^2=\\sum_U(y_i-\\bar y_U)^2/(N-1)$. This design variance does not imply exact normal inference. **B:** zero pair inclusion, census, $n=1$. **Requires:** SME-08; published covariance bilinearity and finite-sum variance. **Sources:** Shalabh Ch. 7 pp. 23–31, Ch. 2 pp. 1–23; Borkowski 6.2 pp. 143–146; Oehlert Ch. 7. Correct Shalabh p.14 sign; exclude Borkowski p.145 PPS arithmetic.\n",
    "after_sme09_block": "### SME-09 — HT variance and SRSWOR\n**Pages:** horvitz-thompson-variance-and-srswor / horvitz-thompson-variance-and-srswor-examples. **A:** Define $\\Delta_{ij}=\\pi_{ij}-\\pi_i\\pi_j$. If every $\\pi_i>0$, prove $ \\operatorname{Var}_d(\\hat T_{HT})=\\sum_{i,j\\in U}\\Delta_{ij}y_iy_j/(\\pi_i\\pi_j)$. The unbiased pair-inclusion estimate $\\sum_{i,j\\in s}(\\Delta_{ij}/\\pi_{ij})(y_i/\\pi_i)(y_j/\\pi_j)$ requires every $\\pi_{ij}>0$; zero pair support does not license division or imply variance estimability. For fixed sample size, $\\sum_j\\Delta_{ij}=0$ yields the Sen–Yates–Grundy identity $-\\tfrac12\\sum_{i,j}\\Delta_{ij}(y_i/\\pi_i-y_j/\\pi_j)^2$; this identity equals the true design variance and is always nonnegative. With positive pair inclusion, its sample inverse-pair form is unbiased. The condition $\\Delta_{ij}\\le0$ for all distinct pairs is sufficient for termwise nonnegative sample estimates, not necessary for a particular fixed population or, in general when $n>2$, for nonnegativity over all population values. For SRSWOR of $n$ from $N>1$, derive $\\operatorname{Cov}_d(I_i,I_j)=-n(N-n)/(N^2(N-1))$ for $i\\ne j$ and $\\operatorname{Var}_d(\\bar y)=(1-n/N)S_U^2/n$, where $S_U^2=\\sum_U(y_i-\\bar y_U)^2/(N-1)$. This design variance does not imply exact normal inference. **B:** zero pair inclusion, census, $n=1$. **Requires:** SME-08; published covariance bilinearity and finite-sum variance. **Sources:** Shalabh Ch. 7 pp. 23–31, Ch. 2 pp. 1–23; Borkowski 6.2 pp. 143–146; Oehlert Ch. 7. Correct Shalabh p.14 sign; exclude Borkowski p.145 PPS arithmetic. Tiwari–Nigam–Pant (2007), Survey Methodology 33(1), pp. 87–94, especially p. 90, confirms that the nonpositive-pair condition is sufficient but not necessary when $n>2$.\n",
    "outside_exact_block_identical": true
  },
